A||N||12||Formula88||Bigger tires on stock rims?||||6||
Z||000000||Formula88||02-17-2001||12:20 PM||||Most everyone has been talking about the biggest wheel/tire combo you can put under your Fiero.  Well, I really love the black lace rims on my Formula, so my question is, what's the widest tire I can go with on the factory rims and not have clearance problems. Stock springs, etc.  Also, I'd like to keep overall tire height as close to stock as possible so I don't throw off the speedo.  I heard someone say they were running 245/50-15's all the way around.  Keep in mind, this is an '88 and has different offset up front.  I was thinking 245/50's on the back and maybe 225/50 or 235/50 on the front (depending on what will fit on the stock rims).  Suggestions?  <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Me&Pat_Cars.jpg"> <BR>John & Pat's Twin '88 Formulas<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000001||Phaeton||02-17-2001||01:35 PM||||The more the tread sticks out beyond the rim the more the vehicle will handle like a boat.<BR>For drag racing only this won't matter and the absolute biggest tire that will fit will give best acceleration.<BR>On 88's the front wheel is 1" narrower than the rear and just to keep consistent front to back wallow the front tires should be 20mm smaller than the rear.<BR>My 88GT has 225 on the rear and 205 on the front.  For tighter handling I was advised to leave the rears at 215 and drop the fronts to 195.  I suspect that was good advice but I like the look of larger tires so a compromise happened.<BR>Without suspension modifications the 88's don't seem to like large tires up front, the nose tends to 'wander' going along straight roads.  The Fiero still takes a set and corners as if on rails, but the wandering on straights gets annoying.<P>NICE pair of Fiero's, heads must turn when they go by.||||reg||12
Z||000002||Formula88||02-17-2001||03:07 PM||||Thanks!  You didn't say if your tires were 60 series or 50's.  Wallow is going to be influenced more by sidewall height than anything else.  But this is why I'm aksing people to post their experiences.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||02-17-2001||08:40 PM||||Formula, that's a lot of tire width on stock wheels. You might be close to interference too, especially on the front on a hard turn and hitting a bump. I might limit the rears to 235-50 and the front to 215-50. That would be a nice compromise with stock springs.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||02-17-2001||11:30 PM||||Well, 215/50's are shorter than the stock tires, and only 10mm wider.  I'm really looking to people's input on what they're running on their car.  What have people had success and failure with, on the stock rims, of course.  Thanks for the input!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fat||02-18-2001||12:12 AM||||225s on the rear didn't work well for me.  With 32lbs of pressure, The middle of the tire wore out quickly.  Those were 225/60/15s on an 88GT.  I went back to 215, and I'm very happy with the original size.  The decreased sidewall height made the car snappier.  Even with 225s on the rear wheel, the sidewall protrusion is noticable enough, when driving, to make the car settle before the tires grip.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||02-18-2001||01:04 AM||||I saw someone at a Fiero meet who, if I remember correctly, had 245/50's all the way around, on 15" rims I believe. But it was a pre-88, and he had aftermarket wheels, so the only thing that might apply is the amount of fenderwell clearance.  I'd really like to step up the width, and keep the height the same.  Since a +1 upsize would be a 225/50-16, would that translate to a 225/55-15 or a 235/50-15 in overall tire diameter?  If I had 16" wheels, I'd just run 225/50-16's on the back and 215-50/16's on the front and that would keep the suspension geometry the same.  Now doing that without changing rims is the trick.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mi Junk||02-18-2001||02:13 AM||||Here is the math for overall tire height.<BR>Factory Tires 215/60/15 = 25.16"<BR>              205/60/15 = 24.68"<BR>              <BR>              195/65/15 = 24.98"<BR>              205/55/15 = 23.88"<BR>              215/55/15 = 24.31"<BR>              225/55/15 = 24.74"<BR>              225/50/15 = 23.86"<BR>              235/50/15 = 24.25"<BR>              245/50/15 = 24.64"<BR>For 16" rims just add 1" to the above measurements.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/85_sig_2.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000008||Formula88||02-18-2001||02:17 AM||||Judging by that, assuming they fit on the rims and in the wheelwells, 245/50-15's all the way around, or with 235's on the front would keep very close to the original suspension geometry and add more rubber.  Thanks!  Has anyone tried these sizes with success?  (I really wish I remembered who I saw at the meet. I can't remember what size rims he had...)||||reg||1
Z||000009||KissMySSFiero||02-18-2001||08:45 AM||||one thing to take into consideration is the width of the tire to the width of the rim.  on a 7 inch rim like the read of your formula.  a 225 is already about as wide as you should go before the sidewall really starts to buldge. i work in a tire store.  the most i would put on a rim is a tire thats about 2 inches wider than the rim.  and another thing to take into consideration is some tires are wider than others.  what i mean by that is a 205/50r15 BFGoodrich tire is wider than a 205/60r15.  yes they both are 205cm wide.  however when you set them side by side you can see a difference.  we have some books at our shop that show the  actual width/heighth of a tire.  goodyear shows it the best.  a 245/50 may be the same height as a 215/60 but the width may be too much for the rim.  when your sidewall is "bowed" in it is weaker therefore you handling suffers, also i believe this can cause some bad wearing problems on the tire.  i think someone already stated something like this.  personally for my formula i wanted to go to a 205/55r15 on the front and 225/50r15 on the rear, this is on stock rims.  <BR>sorry for ramblin on.  i hope some of this makes sense. <P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:KissMySSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:KissMySSFiero@netzero.net">KissMySSFiero@netzero.net</A>   <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">       <BR>"There's a fine line between love and beer"<BR><A HREF="http://expage.com/kissmyssfiero" TARGET=_blank>KissMySSFiero.mywebsite.hehe</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Formula88||02-18-2001||12:17 PM||||Thanks!  What size are you running on your Formula?  Are you sticking with the stock sizes?||||reg||10
Z||000011||gixxer||02-18-2001||09:33 PM||||Formula, save yourself a lot of grief and check out Tirerack.com. They have the technical specs for all the tires they carry. The specs will have the diameters, load capacity and the allowable rim widths, plus more.<BR>My Formula has stock rims with 205/50-15 and 225/50-15 Dunlop SP8000s.<BR>The car would look goofy except that I have lowered it 1 inch all around.<BR> <BR>   ||||reg||1
Z||000012||pontiacs4me||02-19-2001||02:28 AM||||How about some 235/40/17's all the way around.  Im getting billet specialties hollowpoint rims in 17x7  with perfect backspacing to get the edge of the tire at the fender lip.  I plan on lowering it about 1-1.5 inches with eibach pro springs.  How will my clearances be on the front suspension?  Should I go to a little narrower tire on the front, maybe a 225/40?  Im not used to such small tires.  I have 235/60/15's on the front of my bird, and 275/60/15s on the back.  ||||reg||1
